# The sdist is a source distribution: it must be able to reproduce the
# battery, not just the import.
#
# setuptools' default file set picked up tests/test_*.py and left out
# tests/conftest.py, which is where all three tier gates live.  An sdist in
# that shape ships tests that no longer know why they cannot run: the four
# authority tests go back to raising FileNotFoundError on a path inside the
# checkout that has never existed on the reader's machine, which is exactly
# the confusion the gates were built to end.  Docs and tools/battery were
# absent for the same reason, so README's links pointed at nothing.
graft tests
graft docs
graft tools

# `verification` joins them for the same reason and it is the same defect
# caught one directory later: docs/obs-referee.md tells a reader to run
# `verification/fixtures/build_synthetic_suite.py` and to pass
# `verification/manifests/...json` and `verification/vertical-specs/...json`
# on four separate command lines, and docs/native-free-init-admission.md
# passes a vertical spec from the same tree.  Without this line every one of
# those paths is a file the reader does not have, in a document that reads
# as though they should.  Ten files, 66 KB; the schemas and the vertical
# specs are contracts, not receipts.
graft verification

# One file out of `evidence`, and only one.  The receipt files themselves are
# deliberately not distributed, but README.md and docs/source-matrix.md both
# link to `evidence/EVIDENCE.md` -- it is what tells a reader that an
# `evidence/...` reference names the IDENTITY of a measurement rather than a
# file they are missing, and where the contracts those measurements are
# written against actually live.  Without this line those two links resolve to
# nothing in the unpacked sdist, which is exactly the "document points at a
# file the reader does not have" defect the grafts above exist to prevent.
include evidence/EVIDENCE.md

# ...and one directory of tests that must NOT travel, for the same reason
# `[tool.setuptools.packages.find] exclude` drops `hexcore.mod` from the
# wheel.  `graft tests` above is a wildcard, and a wildcard is exactly how the
# thing nobody listed gets shipped: these tests carry the branch name, the
# commit and the SHA-256 digests of a private seam that has no remote and
# exists on one machine.  The package exclusion alone would not stop them --
# they live under tests/, not under the package.
prune tests/mod

# ...and one DOCUMENT that must not travel, caught by the same wildcard logic
# one directory over.  `graft docs` is a wildcard and
# `docs/LANE-BRIEFING.md` is not a user document at all: it is this
# repository's internal working brief, written for whoever picks the tree up
# next.
#
# THE BREAKAGE THIS PREVENTS: an sdist published to PyPI carrying a private
# LAN address, a home-directory path on the maintainer's own machine, the
# maintainer's name against the machines he owns, the reservation protocol
# for those machines, and the existence and card priority of a second,
# unrelated private effort.  None of that is recallable once uploaded, none
# of it helps a reader install or run this software, and the file postdates
# the 0.1.1 sdist -- so this is a leak the previous cut did not have.
# `tests/test_packaging_declaration.py` builds the real sdist and fails if
# the briefing reappears in it.
exclude docs/LANE-BRIEFING.md

# ...and the release checklist, for the same reason and one more.  It is an
# operating procedure for cutting a release of this distribution, not a
# document about using it: it names the private assembly tree, the public
# repository, the maintainer, the scrub policy applied on the way out, and the
# ordered commands for acts that need the maintainer's explicit approval.
# Shipping it inside the artefact it describes would publish the recipe for its
# own publication, and would put governance text into a public tree, which is
# exactly what the 0.1.0 scrub removed by hand.
exclude docs/release-checklist-*.md

# Compiled bytecode is a build product of the sources beside it, never an
# input; the same rule the working tree follows.
global-exclude *.pyc
prune **/__pycache__
